On Linux, RequestServer sends the lookups that it makes with the
system resolver to the UI process, which runs getaddrinfo() for them on
a thread pool, so RequestServer’s sandbox allows none of the resolver
files. Chromium does the same, and only on Linux: Its network service
sends system DNS lookups to the browser process over network::mojom::
SystemDnsResolver, which content::SystemDnsResolverMojoImpl answers on
a thread pool. And ShouldRunOutOfProcessSystemDnsResolution(), in its
ContentBrowserClient, is true only on Linux, since the macOS and
Windows sandboxes can allow system DNS resolution itself.

WebKitGTK runs its network process outside the bubblewrap sandbox, as
bubblewrapSpawn() returns early for ProcessType::Network. And Gecko
resolves names in its parent process, through GetAddrInfo(), which its
nsHostResolver calls. So neither sandboxes the system resolver at all.
